From 8b92b57605303217a33aaf432757c7d886360bfe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PlatinumMimic <julianharmon100@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 19:04:32 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 11/47] Ray tracing: the frame's 3D gathered into an
 acceleration structure, and ambient occlusion traced through it

DXR 1.1 inline ray queries (raytrace.h), on a card that has them, in a runtime built with the Windows SDK's dxc
(rt_*.hlsl to DXIL headers, XBE_RAYTRACING; without either the screen space passes stay).

The scene: each world draw into the frame's 3D that writes depth (not an additive glow) has its vertex shader also
store its vertices as drawn, anti-aliasing's offset and all, into the frame's capture buffer (clip x, y, w, z, its
first texture coordinates, colour, index; root UAV u0 and constants b2). An indexed draw is drawn with its base
vertex added to its indices, so SV_VertexID is its place in the buffers on every driver. Its triangles (strips made
lists) go into one bottom level structure, one instance putting the clip x, y and w back into view space. A cut-out
(alpha tested, its alpha its stage 0 picture's in the fixed function stages) is a non-opaque geometry whose
triangles the passes test against its picture, read from the renderer's SRV heap.

Ambient occlusion (gpu::g_ray_tracing, kRtAmbientOcclusion): a pixel shader drawn into the ambient occlusion's
target. Each pixel's place and normal come from a ray from the eye into the scene (exact at edges), from the depth
where the depth holds something nearer that the scene does not, or at a crack between meshes; it sends 6 rays (3 on
low) over its hemisphere, each hit weighed by its nearness as the screen space pass weighs its samples. The rays
start a few units off the surface, clear of the thin sheets the rooms lay over their floors. About 1 ms at 1080p on
an RTX 5080. XBE_RT_VIEW 1 to 4 show the scene's alignment, the normals, where each place came from and the nearest
hit; XBE_RT_AO_RADIUS, _STRENGTH, _RAYS and _BIAS tune it.

The scene keeps each vertex's texture coordinates and colour and has a list of lights for passes to come.

