From 88fc4cf572f741a6beb48207cbe0cede3c96f8f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PlatinumMimic <julianharmon100@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 19:51:05 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 12/47] Ray traced reflections through the frame's scene;
 device-lost diagnostics (XBE_D3D12_DRED, XBE_D3D12_DEBUG)

Reflections (gpu::kRtReflections, rt_refl.hlsl): a pixel shader over the frame's 3D before its anti-aliasing. Where
the scene is what the frame drew and the draw has a texture pack material, its roughness and metalness (the ORM
map's, or the material's gloss: gpu::Material::gloss, for a pack whose maps are too rough to reflect) give one ray a
pixel along a GGX half vector about its normal (the face's, bent by the normal map), up to 3000 units. What it meets
is that draw's picture times its vertices' colour (the game's light), read as blurred as the cone is wide; Schlick's
Fresnel weighs it, metal's own colour taken out, fading as roughness reaches 0.6. The anti-aliasing gathers the
frames' samples. XBE_RT_VIEW 5 to 7 show what the rays meet, the roughness and the metalness; XBE_RT_REFL_FORCE a
roughness for every material, _ROUGHNESS the limit and _REACH the distance.

The scene keeps each draw's Look: its stage 0 picture (not a render target's), alpha test, material maps and gloss
(GeometryEntry, 48 bytes). Cut-outs are tested at most 32 times a ray (8 for ambient occlusion's short rays, 16 for a
reflection) before the ray ends with what it met. The scene is built once a frame, before the screen space passes:
built after them, a reflections-only frame's build hung the GPU when a room changed.

XBE_D3D12_DRED turns on DRED's breadcrumbs and page faults and, when Present finds the device lost, logs the reason,
the faulting address and where each command list stopped. XBE_D3D12_DEBUG logs the debug layer's errors (the first
64).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01P39J2YaGgWez1ZqKbAmtKk
