From 451bb2881e4734f62f7117414769be6c8236928d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PlatinumMimic <julianharmon100@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 20:50:14 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 14/47] DLSS Ray Reconstruction: ray tracing's light denoised
 by DLSS with the guides

With anti-aliasing on NVIDIA DLSS and Streamline's DLSS-RR files there (sl.dlss_d.dll,
nvngx_dlssd.dll), DLSS Ray Reconstruction takes the frame in DLSS's place on its own
viewport: the guides give it each pixel's albedo, world normal and roughness (packed),
specular albedo (Karis's fit, a texture pack's materials only) and the reflections' hit
distance, which they now write as a second target. The guides' normals are turned into the
world by the view's axes, and its matrices are the view's as its projection has it.
XBE_DLSS_RR=0 keeps DLSS's own anti-aliasing. On a failure it falls back to DLSS for good.

The ray tracing passes take up to four targets (Scene::Out); a frame without a found view
gives DLSS an identity projection rather than zeros.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01P39J2YaGgWez1ZqKbAmtKk
