From ae2518e0281ca113ab6898e4e1755637f629ef59 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PlatinumMimic <julianharmon100@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 08:52:34 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 46/47] Ray traced lighting: no pools from small glows in dark
 rooms, no rings of colour, half the flicker

A surface was raised against the room's average bounce, and a room lit dim all round (5.9's rampart at night) had
an average so low that an item's sparkle by the brazier lit a white pool across the floor and a small glow to the
side a yellow one. What raises a surface is now set against the average or XBE_RT_GI_MEAN_FLOOR (0.15), the more;
what dims one against the average as it is, so the room keeps its brightness.

A surface was raised colour by colour, each against the average's brightness: round the purple brazier each
crossed it at its own distance, rings of pink, yellow and green, and the light's noise came out as blotches of
colour. Now raised by the light's brightness, in its own hue (XBE_RT_GI_HUE, 1).

The bounce lighting at high quality takes four rays a pixel (two), and its denoiser gathers up to 32 frames (24)
and smooths five times (four, out to 16 texels): the flicker on 5.9's floor, twice ambient occlusion alone's, is
now as little as it, for no measurable cost at 1080p.

