From de256c8a0c20da7bc2e5ff74a678ba7e6a65f2c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julian <julianharmon100@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 13:44:53 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Ray traced lighting: a lamp's sprite lit from where it is,
 not from behind its wall; shadow rays blocked by dense cut-outs

Julian: in the Mountains the purple lamps' light bled onto surfaces it shouldn't reach and through objects.

- A light drawn flat (a lamp's flame, a glow's sprite) was put 0.95 of the way to whatever the frame showed at its
  middle: its wall, which is right when the lamp stands before a wall, but a lamp seen against the valley or a
  room's far wall was put out there, and lit that wall all over and the backs of the railings and walls between
  (through them, to the eye). FlatDepth (rt_scene.hlsli): no deeper than the sprite's own depth where that is past 4
  times the near plane (a sprite drawn over everything sits at the near plane), in rt_lights.hlsli's LightEmits and
  rt_shafts.hlsl's LightIn. Measured in the lights-only view (XBE_RT_VIEW=17) on rtleaf's 5.8 and 5.9 lamp spots:
  the purple pixels down from 145 to 2, 216 to 32 and 108 to 23 per ten thousand in the views facing the lamps, the
  dark views unchanged.
- TraceSceneMasked reports nothing when its budget of cut-out tests runs out, so a shadow ray through enough ivy or
  railing let a lamp's light through, and ambient occlusion's rays out. TraceOcclusion (rt_scene.hlsli) takes the
  cut-out at the budget as solid: the shadow rays of LightAt and LightAtKept and ambient occlusion's TraceAo use it;
  the eye's and the bounce rays keep what they met.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01P39J2XTW6h6BAjQxxWwA47
