From 65d96ad61ae9281b374ef013039a416a97a88ab4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julian <julianharmon100@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 17:13:46 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Ray traced lighting: lights known by where they are, flat
 lights at their own depth, the sky left out of the room's average

Julian: in the Baljhet Mountains (his Golem Innocent Devil along) the lighting changed and flickered as he walked a
little further into a room, changed with where the camera looked, changed when Hector attacked, and a cliff face was
lit for no reason.

- A flat light (a flame, a lamp's glow, a floating mote) was put under its sprite in the frame, 0.95 of the way to
  whatever the frame showed at its middle: a mote drifting before a cliff put a point light on the cliff; the golem's
  crystal glow fell on its body from one angle and on the cliff behind from another. The sprites are depth tested, so
  their own depth is their place: FlatPlace (rt_scene.hlsli) puts a flat light's corners at its own depth, none
  within 4 times the near plane or at the far plane (FlatDepth and FlatPixel gone); rt_lights.hlsli's LightEmits and
  rt_shafts.hlsl use it, the middle-on-screen check is gone (a sprite past the edge still lights what is in view).
- The lights' identity was their picture: a glow was a light only when its texture had been drawn 20 frames in a
  row, and its place in the list was its draw order, so a flame whose picture changes, a sprite list the game sorts
  by depth or a torch coming into view moved or reset the lights and the bounce lighting's kept points with them.
  rt_glowmem.hlsl rewritten as the glows' identity: 128 slots matched by position (1.5 x the square root of the
  area, at least 150 units) whatever the picture or the draw order, three passes: the summary (each listed light's
  middle, area, power, the slot that holds it, its streak, its index the frame before, how much of it is grown in),
  the slots' first pass (matched, remembered or freed; row 3 the remap from the frame before's list index to this
  frame's by position) and the second (free slots take unheld lights; row 4 the remembered slots listed compactly).
  LightEmits x LightGrown(li) = saturate((streak - XBE_RT_LIGHTS_STEADY 20) / XBE_RT_LIGHTS_RAMP 20): a swing's
  spark or a burst throws none, a torch all. KeptBefore (rt_gi.hlsl, rt_ao.hlsl) remaps the kept light's index.
  d3d12.cpp: GlowMemory once a frame before ambient occlusion (its lamp shadow needs the remap and the growth),
  LightList in draw order, kGlow 5 targets, XBE_RT_LIGHTS_STEADY_CPU=1 keeps the old CPU steadiness.
- Hector's coat embroidery was a lamp: the glow pass over his skinned mesh is steady, so it lit the floor around him
  in its colour and moved with every swing. A character's glow pass is not a light (XBE_RT_LIGHTS_CHAR=1 restores).
- The sky's light was folded into the room's average (held over 1200 frames): once the camera first showed the sky
  the average rose for twenty seconds and every surface that could not see the sky dimmed over them (view 20 in
  5.0: 0.02 to 0.15 after the camera dipped). rt_gi.hlsl keeps the sky's light out of the average.
- A lift's hue was the light's against white: in a blue night room every cliff and tower over the average went
  deep blue-purple. rt_giapply.hlsl lifts against the room's average's chroma (XBE_RT_GI_HUE_MEAN, clamped 0.25-4).
- Glows gathered along bounce rays were uncapped: a ray through a stack of a golem's glowing quads brought back
  many times what any surface could; capped at lt.w.
- Debug views: 19 the sky held, 20 the room's average, 21 each listed light's box (green a flat light accepted,
  red one at the near plane, blue at the far plane, yellow a 3D light), 22 the growth (red none, green grown, blue
  the streak, a white edge where a slot holds it).

Measured on rtmtn (13.1, 13.4, 13.14 with Julian's save of 5 Nov, the golem along): the golem no longer bathed in
purple, the cliff patch gone, the crystal's glow local; the castle rooms (rtview 5.0, 5.8, 5.9) unchanged as played;
flicker unchanged (1172 to 1166 on rtnoise); the ray tracing at 4K DLSS Balanced 3.64 to 3.73 ms.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01P39J2XTW6h6BAjQxxWwA47
