From bd661e13c4598c7b981efba5a552f935bf1236b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PlatinumMimic <julianharmon100@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 19:45:12 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Ray tracing: the scene as a few merged geometries, built back
 to back; the room's dark parts kept; ambient occlusion at half size at Low

The frame's draws go into the acceleration structure as five or six geometries (the world's solid draws, its
cut-outs, its glows, the characters' solid and cut-out, the water), each draw's triangles in a row in one index buffer
with absolute indices into the capture buffer, and a table of which draw each triangle belongs to (HitOf in
rt_scene.hlsli); a thousand geometries a frame (a room's glows alone were 750) had cost the build more than its
triangles. Each bottom level has its own scratch region and the four build back to back with one barrier after
them (shared, with a barrier between, they were five drains of the GPU a frame): the scene's build went from 0.67 to
0.29 ms at the Legion Go's settings on an RTX 5080. XBE_PERF times the scene's build and the guides apart.

The bounce lighting's fill takes out up to 0.55 of the game's own light (0.7), by the square of how far a surface
falls short of the room's average, so what the rays reach little keeps its picture's detail (the castle's courtyards
and bridge had gone to black); the sky's light is back in the room's average (left out, every surface open to the sky
was raised to twice its brightness), and the average is held over 600 frames (1200).

At the ray tracing's Low quality the ambient occlusion is worked out at half size at any frame size: at the Legion
Go's settings the ray tracing's time went from 1.58 to 1.08 ms.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01P39J2XTW6h6BAjQxxWwA47
