 path's end where its second ray leaves the room, the dark side by the square;
 rooms the rays have nothing to say about left alone

rt_giapply.hlsl: the path traced gain goes out as the lift and the dim do, the part up to 1 in the alpha and the part
over it in the colour: the frame's target clamps what a pixel shader writes to 1, and a gain written whole in the
colour could only darken (the castle rooms came out at half the game's brightness, a torch's wall never raised, and
the purple over a courtyard was the floor's green taken down). Under the average by the square of the shortfall, as
High's dim; the light's hue against the average's to the power XBE_RT_PT_HUE (2), so a brazier's colour spreads over
the floor around it. XBE_RT_GI_SAY (0.02): a room whose average is next to nothing (the Garibaldi Temple's flesh room,
whose walls are skinned meshes, characters to the scene, which the bounce rays pass) is left as the game drew it at
every quality: High had dimmed it to a quarter of its brightness. XBE_RT_VIEW 23 shows the path traced gain.

rt_gi.hlsl: the path's end is the second vertex's game light, and where the second ray leaves the room or goes past
the reach, the first vertex's own (a bridge under the night sky, whose rays off the planks all left the room, got next
to no light and fell to the artists' share); the lights' light along the path capped with the surface's own, its excess
no longer in the room's average. d3d12.cpp: XBE_RT_PT_FILL 0.55 (the artists' share 0.45 at Lighting strength 100%);
RoomBounce can average with the lights' light in (rt_gimean.hlsl's 5; XBE_RT_PT_ALL, 0: off).

